  },
  "locales": {
    "en": {
      "title": "The four seasons",
      "summary": "The seasons and what each one is like, with the comparative of adjectives.",
      "status": "draft",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "In context",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Claire and her friend discuss their favourite season."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "dialog",
              "lines": [
                {
                  "speaker": "Friend",
                  "text": "Which season do you like best?"
                },
                {
                  "speaker": "Claire",
                  "text": "I like spring. It is warmer than winter, and the flowers come out."
                },
                {
                  "speaker": "Friend",
                  "text": "I prefer autumn. The colours are beautiful."
                },
                {
                  "speaker": "Claire",
                  "text": "But summer is the best for holidays."
                },
                {
                  "speaker": "Friend",
                  "text": "It is hotter, and I do not like that."
                },
                {
                  "speaker": "Claire",
                  "text": "Then winter is perfect for you."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Grammar notes",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "**1. The comparative compares two things.** Short adjectives add -er and use than. Longer adjectives use more. See Introduction to Grammar: Adjectives."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Comparative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>warm</strong></td><td><strong>warmer than</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>cold</strong></td><td><strong>colder than</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>hot</strong></td><td><strong>hotter than</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>beautiful</strong></td><td><strong>more beautiful than</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "**2. The superlative picks the top one.** Short adjectives add -est and use the; longer ones use the most. See Introduction to Grammar: Adjectives."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "html": "<table><thead><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Superlative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>warm</strong></td><td><strong>the warmest</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>cold</strong></td><td><strong>the coldest</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>interesting</strong></td><td><strong>the most interesting</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Notice and culture note",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "The four seasons are **spring**, **summer**, **autumn** and **winter**.",
                "Americans say **fall** instead of autumn.",
                "Seasons are not capitalised: **in spring**, **in winter**."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraphs",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Britain has a mild climate with no extreme heat or cold. In much of the United States the four seasons are more marked."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
